" (https://cronfa.swan.ac.uk/Record/cronfa18053/Download/0018053-15042015121236.pdf)\nciteturn13view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 6; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cronfa.swan.ac.uk/Record/cronfa18053/Download/0018053-15042015121236.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 420\nL0@P0: Cronfa - Swansea University Open Access Repository\nL1@P0: \nL2@P0: _____________________________________________________________\nL3@P0: \nL4@P0: This is an author produced version of a paper published in:\nL5@P0: IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters\nL6@P0: \nL7@P0: \nL8@P0: Cronfa URL for this paper:\nL9@P0: http://cronfa.swan.ac.uk/Record/cronfa18053\nL10@P0: _____________________________________________________________\nL11@P0: Paper:\nL12@P0: Najim, S., Lim, I., Wittek, P. & Jones, M. (2014). FSPE: Visualization of Hyperspectral Imagery Using Faithful\nL13@P0: Stochastic Proximity Embedding. IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters, 12(1), 18-22.\nL14@P0: http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/LGRS.2014.2324631\nL15@P0: _____________________________________________________________\nL16@P0: \nL17@P0: This item is brought to you by Swansea University. Any person downloading material is agreeing to abide by the terms\nL18@P0: of the repository licence. Copies of full text items may be used or reproduced in any format or medium, without prior\nL19@P0: permission for personal research or study, educational or non-commercial purposes only. The copyright for any work\nL20@P0: remains with the original author unless otherwise specified. The full-text must not be sold in any format or medium\nL21@P0: without the formal permission of the copyright holder.\nL22@P0: Permission for multiple reproductions should be obtained from the original author.\nL23@P0: Authors are personally responsible for adhering to copyright and publisher restrictions when uploading content to the\nL24@P0: repository.\nL25@P0-1: http://www.swansea.ac.uk/library/researchsupport/ris-support/ 18 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 1, JANUARY 2015\nL26@P1: FSPE: Visualization of Hyperspectral Imagery Using\nL27@P1: Faithful Stochastic Proximity Embedding\nL28@P1: Safa A. Najim, Student Member, IEEE, Ik Soo Lim, Peter Wittek, and Mark W. Jones\nL29@P1: Abstract—Hyperspectral image visualization reduces color\nL30@P1: bands to three, but prevailing linear methods fail to address\nL31@P1: data characteristics, and nonlinear embeddings are computa\u0002tionally demanding. Qualitative evaluation of embedding is also\nL32@P1: lacking. We propose faithful stochastic proximity embedding\nL33@P1: (FSPE), which is a scalable and nonlinear dimensionality re\u0002duction method. FSPE considers the nonlinear characteristics of\nL34@P1: spectral signatures, yet it avoids the costly computation of geodesic\nL35@P1: distances that are often required by other nonlinear methods.\nL36@P1: Furthermore, we employ a pixelwise metric that measures the\nL37@P1: quality of hyperspectral image visualization at each pixel. FSPE\nL38@P1: outperforms the state-of-art methods by at least 12% on average\nL39@P1: and up to 25% in the qualitative measure. An implementation\nL40@P1: on graphics processing units is two orders of magnitude faster\nL41@P1: than the baseline. Our method opens the path to high-fidelity and\nL42@P1: real-time analysis of hyperspectral images.\nL43@P1: Index Terms—Dimension reduction methods, hyperspectral im\u0002agery sensing, visualization.\nL44@P1: I. INTRODUCTION\nL45@P1: HYPERSPECTRAL images are useful in examining the\nL46@P1: compounds and elements on the surface of the Earth,\nL47@P1: which are important for various geoscience-related applica\u0002tions. However, it is challenging to process and analyze hy\u0002perspectral images due to the high dimensionality of hundreds\nL48@P1: of spectral bands (> 200) per pixel position. Thus, prior to\nL49@P1: the classification or visualization of hyperspectral images, one\nL50@P1: often needs to convert high-dimensional data to a lower dimen\u0002sion while preserving the main features of the original space.\nL51@P1: For this dimensionality reduction, principal component anal\u0002ysis (PCA) is one of the most widely used methods [1], [2].\nL52@P1: However, assuming the linearity of the underlying manifold,\nL53@P1: PCA and similar linear projection methods fail to capture\nL54@P1: the nonlinear characteristics of hyperspectral imagery. Thus,\nL55@P1: these methods are prone to significant estimation errors [3]. To\nL56@P1: avoid the pitfalls of linear methods, nonlinear dimensionality\nL57@P1: Manuscript received October 15, 2013; revised January 15, 2014; accepted\nL58@P1: March 25, 2014.\nL59@P1: S. A. Najim is with the School of Computer Science, College of Physical\nL60@P1: and Applied Sciences, Bangor University, LL57 2DG Gwynedd, U.K., and\nL61@P1: also with the Department of Computer Science, College of Science, Basrah\nL62@P1: University, Basrah, Iraq (e-mail: safanajim@gmail.com).\nL63@P1: I. S. Lim is with the School of Computer Science, College of Physical\nL64@P1: and Applied Sciences, Bangor University, LL57 2DG Gwynedd, U.K. (e-mail:\nL65@P1: i.s.lim@bangor.ac.uk).\nL66@P1: P. Wittek is with the University of Borås, 501 90 Borås, Sweden (e-mail:\nL67@P1: peterwittek@acm.org).\nL68@P1: M. W. Jones is with Swansea University, SA2 8PP Swansea, U.K.\nL69@P1: (e-mail: m.w.jones@swansea.ac.uk).\nL70@P1: Color versions of one or more of the figures in this paper are available online\nL71@P1: at http://ieeexplore.ieee.org.\nL72@P1: Digital Object Identifier 10.1109/LGRS.2014.2324631\nL73@P1: Fig. 1. Inevitable distortions in reducing the dimensionality to three or less.\nL74@P1: The dimensionality of a hyperspectral image is reduced to 10, 9,..., 3, 2, or 1\nL75@P1: using various methods of dimensionality reduction. The quality of the dimen\u0002sionality reduction is measured with a correlation metric between interpoint\nL76@P1: distances in the input data space and the projected space [1], [9]; the higher\nL77@P1: the correlation, the better. Regardless of the methods used for dimensionality\nL78@P1: reduction, the correlation at dimension 3 is lower than that at 10, implying\nL79@P1: inevitable distortions in the color display of hyperspectral images.\nL80@P1: reduction methods have attracted much attention. Examples\nL81@P1: include isometric feature mapping (ISOMAP, see [3] and [4]),\nL82@P1: and locally linear embedding (LLE, see [5] and [6]), which\nL83@P1: preserve the nonlinear features of the underlying manifold.\nL84@P1: Dimensionality reduction for classification or segmentation\nL85@P1: applications seeks a low and intrinsic dimensionality, for in\u0002stance, ten bands [5]. Dimensionality reduction for the color\nL86@P1: display of hyperspectral images is even more constrained: the\nL87@P1: reduced dimensionality cannot be more than three, i.e., the\nL88@P1: number of color bands, regardless of the data characteristics\nL89@P1: [1], [7]. By intrinsic dimensionality we mean the minimal\nL90@P1: number of variables necessary to account for all the pairwise\nL91@P1: distances in the data [8]. When the intrinsic dimensionality of\nL92@P1: hyperspectral images is higher than three, the dimensionality\nL93@P1: reduction for the color display inevitably leads to distortions\nL94@P1: or loss of spectral information and, thus, to unfaithful colors.\nL95@P1: For instance, distant points in the high-dimensional data space\nL96@P1: might be projected to nearby points in the color space. In\nL97@P1: other words, pixels of dissimilar spectral signatures will have\nL98@P1: similar colors, making the visualization unfaithful or unreliable.\nL99@P1: Fig. 1 shows that a reduction to three dimensions or less leads\nL100@P1: to lower accuracy than a reduction to higher dimensions. The\nL101@P1: resulting color display will contain inevitable distortions and\nL102@P1: misleading colors.\nL103@P1: By proposing faithful stochastic proximity embedding\nL104@P1: (FSPE), we reduce misleading colors to make the color display\nL105@P1: of hyperspectral images more reliable or faithful. FSPE is a\nL106@P1: 1545-598X © 2014 IEEE. Personal use is permitted, but republication/redistribution requires IEEE permission.\nL107@P1-2: See http://www.ieee.org/publications_standards/publications/rights/index.html for more information.NAJIM et al.: FSPE: VISUALIZATION OF HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGERY USING FSPE 19\nL108@P2: novel dimensionality reduction method that leads to a more\nL109@P2: faithful color display than other popular methods such as PCA,\nL110@P2: ISOMAP, and LLE. Apart from a higher quality embedding,\nL111@P2: FSPE also has a computational advantage: it is easy to paral\u0002lelize, and graphics processing unit (GPU)-based implemen\u0002tation enables interactive applications. Furthermore, we use a\nL112@P2: pixelwise metric of good visualization. Unlike the other metrics\nL113@P2: that only provide a global measure of the whole visualization\nL114@P2: with a single number [1], [9], the pixelwise metric provides a\nL115@P2: quantitative measure for each pixel of the hyperspectral images\nL116@P2: and tells us how reliable the color is per pixel. This measure\nL117@P2: is a more stringent criterion to estimate the quality of the\nL118@P2: projection. FSPE outperforms competing methods by a large\nL119@P2: margin using both the traditional correlation measure and the\nL120@P2: qualitative measure.\nL121@P2: II. DIMENSIONALITY REDUCTION\nL122@P2: For every data point xi ∈ Rh in a high-dimensional space\nL123@P2: (e.g., pixels in a hyperspectral image with h bands), the dimen\u0002sionality reduction seeks its low-dimensional representation\nL124@P2: yi ∈ Rl\nL125@P2: , where l<h (e.g., l = 3 for the color display of a\nL126@P2: hyperspectral image). Dimensionality reduction is often cast as\nL127@P2: a minimization problem with the following cost function:\nL128@P2: f = \u0002\nL129@P2: i\nL130@P2: \u0002\nL131@P2: j\u0003=i\nL132@P2: (rij − dij )\nL133@P2: 2W(·) (1)\nL134@P2: where rij is the distance between xi and xj in the input\nL135@P2: data space, and dij is the Euclidean distance \u0004yi − yj\u0004 in\nL136@P2: the projection space of reduced dimensionality. For weighting\nL137@P2: function W(·), various forms exist [10].\nL138@P2: With W(·)=1, PCA and classical multidimensional scaling\nL139@P2: use the Euclidean distance for rij [11]. The Euclidean distance\nL140@P2: underestimates the proximity of data points on a nonlinear man\u0002ifold and leads to erroneous dimensionality reduction. ISOMAP\nL141@P2: addresses this problem by using the geodesic distance for rij ,\nL142@P2: which better captures the nonlinear features of the data [4].\nL143@P2: With W(dij )=1 if dij ≤ dc and W(dij )=0 if dij > dc\nL144@P2: for a neighbor radius dc, curvilinear component analysis uses\nL145@P2: Euclidean distances for rij but focuses on preserving the\nL146@P2: distances between nearby low-dimensional representations yi\nL147@P2: [12]. Curvilinear distance analysis (CDA) is similar, except that\nL148@P2: it uses geodesic distances for rij [13].\nL149@P2: III. FSPE\nL150@P2: A. SPE\nL151@P2: ISOMAP has to estimate the geodesic distances between data\nL152@P2: points, which is then followed by the eigendecomposition of\nL153@P2: the distance matrix. The distance computation and the eigende\u0002composition incur a substantial computing time. Although more\nL154@P2: efficient versions of ISOMAP are available [3], [14], it is still\nL155@P2: too slow, particularly for interactive visualization [1].\nL156@P2: As the geodesic distance is always greater than or equal to\nL157@P2: its corresponding Euclidean distance, stochastic proximity em\u0002bedding (SPE) takes the Euclidean distances between remote\nL158@P2: points as the lower bounds of their true geodesic distances and\nL159@P2: uses them as a means to impose global structure [15]. Avoiding\nL160@P2: the computation of geodesic distances between remote points,\nL161@P2: SPE merely requires that the distances between their low\u0002dimensional representations do not fall below their Euclidean\nL162@P2: distances in the high-dimensional input data space.\nL163@P2: Starting with a random initial configuration, the SPE algo\u0002rithm iteratively refines the embedding by repeatedly selecting\nL164@P2: two points i and j at random and adjusting their coordinates in\nL165@P2: a manner similar to a stochastic gradient descent as follows:\nL166@P2: yi ← yi + λ(t)S(rij )\nL167@P2: rij − dij\nL168@P2: dij + \u0003 (yi − yj )\nL169@P2: yj ← yj + λ(t)S(rij )\nL170@P2: rij − dij\nL171@P2: dij + \u0003 (yj − yi) (2)\nL172@P2: S(rij ) =\nL173@P2: ⎧\nL174@P2: ⎪⎪⎪⎨\nL175@P2: ⎪⎪⎪⎩\nL176@P2: 1, if rij ≤ rc\nL177@P2: or\nL178@P2: if rij > rc and rij > dij ,\nL179@P2: 0, otherwise\nL180@P2: (3)\nL181@P2: where λ(t) is the learning rate that decreases over time t, \u0003 is\nL182@P2: a tiny number used to avoid division by zero, rij is Euclidean\nL183@P2: distance \u0004xi − xj\u0004, and rc is the neighborhood radius.\nL184@P2: Preserving the nonlinear geometry of complex high\u0002dimensional data, SPE is a fast and scalable method for di\u0002mensionality reduction. SPE, however, suffers from distortions\nL185@P2: when reducing dimensionality below the intrinsic dimensional\u0002ity. As with other methods of dimensionality reduction, remote\nL186@P2: points in the input data space can end up as nearby points in the\nL187@P2: projection space.\nL188@P2: B. FSPE\nL189@P2: To make SPE more faithful in visualization, we propose the\nL190@P2: FSPE algorithm as follows:\nL191@P2: yi ← yi + λ(t)T(dij )\nL192@P2: rij − dij\nL193@P2: dij + \u0003 (yi − yj )\nL194@P2: yj ← yj + λ(t)T(dij )\nL195@P2: rij − dij\nL196@P2: dij + \u0003 (yj − yi) (4)\nL197@P2: T(dij ) =\nL198@P2: ⎧\nL199@P2: ⎪⎪⎪⎨\nL200@P2: ⎪⎪⎪⎩\nL201@P2: 1, if dij ≤ dc(t)\nL202@P2: or\nL203@P2: if dij > dc(t) and dij < rij ,\nL204@P2: 0, otherwise\nL205@P2: (5)\nL206@P2: where dc(t) is a neighbor radius that decreases over time, and\nL207@P2: rij is Euclidean distance \u0004xi − xj\u0004. Starting with a random\nL208@P2: initial configuration, the proposed FSPE algorithm iteratively\nL209@P2: refines it by repeatedly selecting two points i and j at random\nL210@P2: and adjusts their coordinates according to (4).\nL211@P2: The key difference between SPE and FSPE is in S(rij ) and\nL212@P2: T(dij ). Whereas the S(rij ) of SPE is a function of distance rij\nL213@P2: in the input data space with a fixed neighborhood radius rc, the\nL214@P2: T(dij ) of FSPE is a function of distance dij in the projection\nL215@P2-3: space with a time-varying neighborhood radius dc(t). T(dij )20 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 1, JANUARY 2015\nL216@P3: Fig. 2. We use 12 image patches for the experiments, which contain various spectral signatures, covering Moffett Field, California at the southern end of\nL217@P3: San Francisco Bay. The visualization was carried out using the proposed FSPE.\nL218@P3: makes the projection of dimensionality reduction more faithful\nL219@P3: in the following senses.\nL220@P3: 1) It detects those “unfaithful” points that are pairwise re\u0002mote in the input data space (i.e., large rij ) but that are\nL221@P3: nearby in the projection space (i.e., small dij ), the latter\nL222@P3: of which satisfies the condition dij ≤ dc(t) in (5).\nL223@P3: 2) It corrects the unfaithful points to make them more faith\u0002ful, i.e., the large discrepancy rij − dij adjusts them to be\nL224@P3: further apart in the projection space according to (4).\nL225@P3: C. Local Enhancement by FSPE\nL226@P3: Even after the completion of the projection, the FSPE algo\u0002rithm is applied with a different purpose. After the projection, a\nL227@P3: user interactively selects points of interest {i1, i2,...,ip} and\nL228@P3: reruns the FSPE algorithm in (4). Taking the final configuration\nL229@P3: as the new initial configuration, the update selects j at random\nL230@P3: from the set of the whole data points, but it is limited in select\u0002ing i ∈ {i1, i2,...,ip}. This local enhancement will improve\nL231@P3: the projection quality of the selected points, i.e., more reliable\nL232@P3: colors will be assigned to them.\nL233@P3: Local enhancement by FSPE applies to any dimensional\u0002ity reduction method. If one already has a configuration of\nL234@P3: points of reduced dimensionality, for instance, by PCA, then\nL235@P3: one carries out the local enhancement of selected points by\nL236@P3: FSPE, starting with the PCA-based configuration as an initial\nL237@P3: configuration.\nL238@P3: IV. PIXELWISE QUALITY METRIC\nL239@P3: A popular choice for estimating the quality of dimensionality\nL240@P3: reduction is a metric based on the following correlation coeffi\u0002cient [1], [9]:\nL241@P3: γ = XT Y /|X| − X¯Y¯\nL242@P3: std(X)std(Y ) (6)\nL243@P3: where X is the vector of all pairwise distances of the data points\nL244@P3: in the input data space, and Y is the vector of the corresponding\nL245@P3: pairwise Euclidean distances in the projection space. However,\nL246@P3: summarizing the quality of the dimensionality reduction for the\nL247@P3: entire image with a single number, this global metric cannot\nL248@P3: assess the quality of the dimensionality reduction at individual\nL249@P3: pixels. This widely varies among different pixels in a single\nL250@P3: image.\nL251@P3: To remedy this issue, we use a pixelwise correlation metric\nL252@P3: as follows:\nL253@P3: γ(i) = XT\nL254@P3: i Yi/|Xi| − X¯iY¯i\nL255@P3: std(Xi)std(Yi) (7)\nL256@P3: where Xi is the vector of all pairwise distances involving the\nL257@P3: ith pixel in the input data space, and Yi is the vector of the\nL258@P3: corresponding pairwise Euclidean distances in the projection\nL259@P3: space.\nL260@P3: V. EXPERIMENTAL RESULTS\nL261@P3: We implemented our method in Microsoft Visual Studio C++\nL262@P3: 2008 with CUDA 4.2 in Windows 7. The hardware included an\nL263@P3: Intel i7-930 CPU clocked at 2.80 GHz, with 12 GB of main\nL264@P3: memory. The graphics processor was an Nvidia GTX 280 with\nL265@P3: a buffer size of 1 GB. We use 12 hyperspectral images that have\nL266@P3: various spectral signatures (see Fig. 2).\nL267@P3: A. Correlation Metric\nL268@P3: We tested the performance of FSPE and compared it against\nL269@P3: other projection methods using correlation metric γ. ISOMAP,\nL270@P3: LLE, and SPE involve free parameters such as the number of\nL271@P3: the neighbors k or neighbor radius rc. For a fair comparison,\nL272@P3: we tried different values of k and rc and only included the best\nL273@P3: result for each of these methods.\nL274@P3: The proposed FSPE is competitive, frequently outperforming\nL275@P3: the other methods. Among the 12 test images, FSPE outper\u0002forms all the other methods with five images and comes as\nL276@P3: the second best performer with three images (see Table I). The\nL277@P3: average performance is 12% better. Note that, since FSPE out\u0002performs PCA, we do not compare against other methods that\nL278@P3: are known to be similar to or outperformed by PCA [16], [17].\nL279@P3: For FSPE, we use \u0003 = 1.0 × 10−8. The initial configuration\nL280@P3: is a random distribution of points in a 3-D cube [0, 1] × [0, 1] ×\nL281@P3: [0, 1]. To avoid oscillatory behavior, learning rate λ(t) linearly\nL282@P3-4: decreases from λ0 = 1.0 to λ1 = 1.0 × 10−3.NAJIM et al.: FSPE: VISUALIZATION OF HYPERSPECTRAL IMAGERY USING FSPE 21\nL283@P4: TABLE I\nL284@P4: PERFORMANCE OF DIMENSIONALITY REDUCTION PROJECTIONS\nL285@P4: MEASURED ACCORDING TO CORRELATION COEFFICIENT γ.\nL286@P4: THE HIGHER, THE BETTER\nL287@P4: Fig. 3. Grayscale images of pixelwise correlation values γ(i). The top row\nL288@P4: shows the color display of a hyperspectral image by PCA, ISOMAP, and FSPE.\nL289@P4: The bottom row presents the corresponding grayscale display of the pixelwise\nL290@P4: correlation values; the brighter, the better (i.e., higher). (a) PCA, γ = 0.691.\nL291@P4: (b) ISOMAP, γ = 0.525. (c) FSPE, γ = 0.863.\nL292@P4: B. Faithful Visualization\nL293@P4: For visualization of hyperspectral images, we use the CIE\nL294@P4: L∗a∗b∗ color space that is perceptually uniform. Among the\nL295@P4: three coordinates in the projection space, we choose the most\nL296@P4: varying coordinate for the L∗ component and the remaining two\nL297@P4: coordinates for the a∗ and b∗ components, followed by a simple\nL298@P4: linear stretching such that L∗ ∈ [0, 100] and that both a∗ and b∗\nL299@P4: have a zero mean.\nL300@P4: Not only providing a quantitative measure of the projection\nL301@P4: quality at each pixel, pixelwise correlation metric γ(i) is also\nL302@P4: useful during the color display and visual examination of hyper\u0002spectral images. In addition to a hyperspectral image in color,\nL303@P4: one may display a supplementary grayscale image. This image\nL304@P4: indicates the reliability of the color at each pixel i by mapping\nL305@P4: γ(i) to a grayscale intensity, i.e., the brighter, the higher (i.e.,\nL306@P4: more reliable) (see Fig. 3). This provides extra information that\nL307@P4: the overall correlation metric γ is unable to provide. Although\nL308@P4: the overall correlation value γ by PCA is higher than that by\nL309@P4: ISOMAP for example, the grayscale display shows that PCA\nL310@P4: yields pixels with γ(i) values that are significantly lower (i.e.,\nL311@P4: darker) than those of the corresponding pixels by ISOMAP (see\nL312@P4: Fig. 3). At these pixels, the quality of dimensionality reduction\nL313@P4: or the reliability of color by PCA is lower than that by ISOMAP.\nL314@P4: Fig. 4. Visualization results of a large hyperspectral image of 1800 ×\nL315@P4: 600 pixels by PCA, SPE, and FSPE.\nL316@P4: Fig. 4 shows the visualization results of a large hyperspectral\nL317@P4: image (1800 × 600 pixels) by PCA, SPE, and FSPE; the\nL318@P4: proposed FSPE outperforms the others.\nL319@P4: C. Local Enhancement\nL320@P4: To demonstrate the local enhancement by FSPE, we choose\nL321@P4: the upper-right part of a hyperspectral image as a region of\nL322@P4: interest; the dimensionality reduction of the image has been\nL323@P4: initially carried out by CDA, which yields a low correlation\nL324@P4: value for the region. Thus, the pixel colors of the region are\nL325@P4: unreliable and even misleading. After the local enhancement\nL326@P4: by FSPE, the correlation value significantly increases, and the\nL327@P4: new colors of the corresponding pixels are more reliable and\nL328@P4: faithful (see Fig. 5).\nL329@P4: D. GPU Implementation\nL330@P4: Nonlinear dimensionality reduction is often too slow to\nL331@P4: compute for interactive visualization. Linear methods, such\nL332@P4: as PCA, are used due to their fast computation [1]. FSPE is\nL333@P4: competitive in computational time. For a hyperspectral image\nL334@P4: of 300 × 300 pixels with 224 spectral bands, the computation\nL335@P4: of FSPE only takes 12.416 s on a CPU; the total number of\nL336@P4: pairwise refinement steps is 9 × 107. Owing to its inherently\nL337@P4: parallel nature, the computation time of FSPE is further reduced\nL338@P4: when benefiting from the parallel computing power of low\u0002cost GPUs. The computation of FSPE takes 0.011 s on a GPU,\nL339@P4: yielding a speed up of two orders of magnitude and making it\nL340@P4-5: suitable for interactive visualization.22 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 1, JANUARY 2015\nL341@P5: Fig. 5. Local enhancement by FSPE. (a) Color display of a hyperspectral\nL342@P5: image by CDA and its corresponding grayscale image of pixelwise correlation\nL343@P5: γ(i). The upper-right part of it is chosen as a region of interest. Since the corre\u0002lation value of the region is low (γ = 0.036), its colors appearing (relatively)\nL344@P5: homogeneous may not be reliable and misleadingly implies the homogeneity\nL345@P5: of spectral signatures. (b) After the local enhancement by FSPE, the correlation\nL346@P5: value of the region significantly increases to γ = 0.890; thus, its colors are now\nL347@P5: more reliable, showing more heterogeneity of spectral signatures.\nL348@P5: Fig. 6. Computation time of the FSPE algorithm for hyperspectral images\nL349@P5: with different sizes. The GPU implementation maintains a speed up of two\nL350@P5: orders of magnitude compared with the CPU implementation.\nL351@P5: As other nonlinear projection methods often involve the\nL352@P5: eigendecomposition or computation of geodesic distances, they\nL353@P5: are more challenging to speed up their computation by GPUs.\nL354@P5: Fig. 6 shows the running time of the GPU implementation\nL355@P5: of FSPE compared with the CPU implementation for differ\u0002ent data sizes, i.e., 1 × 300 × 300, 2 × 300 × 300,..., 12 ×\nL356@P5: 300 × 300 pixels; for a hyperspectral image of a million pixels,\nL357@P5: the implementation of FSPE on a GPU takes less than 16 s.\nL358@P5: VI. CONCLUSION\nL359@P5: FSPE is a novel nonlinear dimensionality reduction method\nL360@P5: for visualizing hyperspectral imagery. While considering the\nL361@P5: nonlinear characteristics of data points in the high-dimensional\nL362@P5: spectral space, FSPE avoids the costly computation of explicitly\nL363@P5: estimating geodesic distances that are often required by other\nL364@P5: nonlinear methods. The experimental results prove that FSPE is\nL365@P5: competitive with or outperforms state-of-art methods in hyper\u0002spectral image visualization. We also demonstrate that, due to\nL366@P5: its parallel nature, FSPE takes advantage of parallel computing\nL367@P5: devices such as GPUs, leading to substantial speed ups and\nL368@P5: interactive applications. We also utilize a pixelwise metric that\nL369@P5: measures the quality of hyperspectral image visualization at\nL370@P5: individual pixels. The future work includes an optimized GPU\nL371@P5: implementation of FSPE aiming at processing a hyperspectral\nL372@P5: image of a million pixels within a second or so and improving\nL373@P5: the pixelwise metric with a weighted version. The source code\nL374@P5: of FSPE is available [18].\nL375@P5: REFERENCES\nL376@P5: [1] M. Cui, A. Razdan, J. Hu, and P. Wonka, “Interactive hyperspectral image\nL377@P5: visualization using convex optimization,” IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote\nL378@P5: Sens., vol. 47, no. 6, pp. 1673–1684, Jun. 2009.\nL379@P5: [2] J. S. Tyo, A. Konsolakis, D. I. Diersen, and R. C. Olsen, “Principal\u0002components-based display strategy for spectral imagery,” IEEE Trans.\nL380@P5: Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 41, no. 3, pp. 708–718, Mar. 2003.\nL381@P5: [3] C. M. Bachmann, T. L. Ainsworth, and R. A. Fusina, “Improved mani\u0002fold coordinate representations of large-scale hyperspectral scenes,” IEEE\nL382@P5: Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 44, no. 10, pp. 2786–2803, Oct. 2006.\nL383@P5: [4] J. B. Tenenbaum, V. de Silva, and J. C. Langford, “A global geometric\nL384@P5: framework for nonlinear dimensionality reduction,” Science, vol. 290,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nuntitled (https://www2.umbc.edu/rssipl/people/aplaza/Papers/Journals/2015.GRSL.SVMSubspace.pdf)\nciteturn13view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 5; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www2.umbc.edu/rssipl/people/aplaza/Papers/Journals/2015.GRSL.SVMSubspace.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 421\n"
